{
  "review": {
    "summary": "I found no BLOCKER, MAJOR, MINOR or NIT issues in the supplied scope, and the three items from the last review look fixed. (1) There is now one shared capacity check, `assertViewerCapacity`. Both the new-session path and the expired-renewal path call it, so the duplicated check is gone. (2) The capacity check counts with the same active filter as before (`expiresAt > now`), and the expired check uses `expiresAt <= now`. The two boundaries match, so an expired session never counts against itself. An active renewal skips the check and keeps its slot. (3) The check runs inside the same Serializable transaction that is retried on P2034. The test barrier holds both transactions until each has read a count of zero, so the overlap is real. In PostgreSQL, both reading the same filter and then writing rows that match it should force one transaction to abort with P2034. The losing retry should then read a count of 1 and fail with `viewer_limit_reached`. The checks `reads >= 3`, exactly one success, that rejection code and one active session together support that. If either transaction skipped its count, the barrier would hang and the test would fail rather than pass falsely. Things I couldn't confirm from the excerpt: (a) the expired-renewal branch ends after its update and doesn't fall through to the second check at line 242 (if it did, there would be a redundant count, not a security hole); (b) `LIVE_ADMISSION_MAX_ATTEMPTS` is at least 2; (c) the final count in the test uses the real `new Date()` while the code uses a mocked date, which is fine only if the mocked time is not later than real time; (d) whether any test covers an active renewal at full capacity (should still succeed) or a session expiring at exactly `now`. These are confirmation points, not reported defects. No findings does not mean omitted code, the full test suite or the release is certified.",
